# Next.js + Supabase conventions

## Stack defaults

- Next.js App Router (not Pages Router unless the file already uses it)
- TypeScript strict; avoid `any`
- Supabase via `@supabase/ssr` for auth and server/client clients

## Server vs client

- Default to Server Components — no `'use client'` unless the component needs hooks, browser APIs, or event handlers
- Never import server-only modules (e.g. `next/headers`, server Supabase client) into client components
- Fetch data in RSC or server actions; don't duplicate fetches in `useEffect` when RSC can do it

## Supabase

- Browser: anon key only (`NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`)
- Server: use cookie-aware `createServerClient`; service role key only in server-only code, never `NEXT_PUBLIC_`
- Respect RLS — do not use service role to bypass policies from user-facing code
- Prefer generated `Database` types from `supabase gen types`

## Project layout

- `app/` — routes, layouts, server actions colocated with features when small
- `components/` — shared UI; `components/ui/` for primitives
- `lib/supabase/` — client factories (`server.ts`, `client.ts`, `middleware.ts`)
- `lib/` — shared utilities and data-access helpers

## Changes

- Smallest correct diff; don't refactor unrelated code
- No new dependencies without explicit ask
- Update `.env.example` when adding env vars; never commit real secrets
- Match existing naming, formatting, and patterns in the file you're editing

## When unsure

- Ask which Supabase client (server vs browser) before writing queries
- Prefer explicit error handling over empty catch blocks
